<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Heqanakht</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 1950 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century-bce">20th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Heqanakht was an Egyptian landowner and funerary priest of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-francia">Middle Kingdom</a>, known from a group of letters and accounts found in a tomb near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bahari">Deir el-Bahari</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>. Written while he was away from home, the Heqanakht Papyri give detailed instructions on fields, rents, rations and family quarrels. They are among the most important sources for daily life and the rural economy of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p></div>
